    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_7d3430febb7ef08b2c05ce09bb9c652eae7023d289b95553c888f6d945e4b06c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For positive, monotonically increasing v on the half-line, replacing x by a velocity profile does not destroy the formal eigenfunction structure: the coordinate q(x)=∫dx/v(x) carries a plane-wave phase, while v(x)^(-1/2) carries the profile-dependent amplitude. This separates translation-like phase evolution in q from the local weighting imposed by v, and v=x recovers the dilation case. The gain is an adapted representation for a whole class of flows; the loss is that the result remains a formal continuum statement and does not by itself provide operator domains, boundary conditions, or a discrete spectrum.",
