    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_ab5d4dfa3e0a2f411aea9ce504966b1e2738902b35c6f702b2db3e933f04bebd",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "One-particle solvability comes from a statewise threshold representation: the inverse-model eigenvalue is moved into RD couplings at zero energy, after which subtracting adjacent threshold equations gives a local recursion and boundary closure gives the global product quantization condition. The same mechanism marks its own many-body boundary, because several rapidities prevent the single-parameter I-to-RD map from extending directly.",
